issues.problem:
Delimiter detection counts physical lines (text split on newlines) that contain each candidate, so it does not see quotes. When quoted multi-line fields have continuation lines that contain ';' but no ',', the semicolon wins, and every csv/txt import silently produces garbled cards with the wrong count. This includes Nib's own study.exportCSV output, even though StudyExport says StudyImport reads the file back as-is. Reproduced by porting the logic: exporting the rows ["Sum 0..n in C", "int s = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    s += i;
return s;"] and ["Swap a and b", "t = a;
a = b;
b = t;"] gives hits {',': 2, ';': 7}, so ';' is chosen for both .csv and .txt. Code, chemistry and multi-line Excel cells hit this. The reverse also fails: a semicolon CSV whose multi-line cells contain commas. The round-trip test only uses single-line semicolons, so CI misses it.
